1
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:18,177
At first glance, the salt pan reads as sterile. Halite
crust, volcanic rock, shallow brine. Then movement.

2
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:26,301
This is Laguna Chaxa on Chile’s Salar de
Atacama. Let’s stay with the water awhile.

3
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:34,393
How does a hypersaline basin
sustain a flock of Chilean flamingos?

4
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:54,720
Look ahead and slightly right. Watch
the mechanics of that downward-bent bill.

5
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:05,540
The bird lowers its neck, inverting the
upper mandible just beneath the surface.

6
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:27,186
Fine internal lamellae strain brine shrimp and
diatoms as tongue strokes pump water out the sides.

7
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:35,242
The prey stays microscopic; the foraging
behaviour makes the food web visible.

8
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:46,257
Watch a single step. Concentric surface
waves travel clean across the sky reflection.

9
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:05,991
Farther out, another flamingo balances on
one leg with the other tucked into its plumage.

10
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:16,453
Foraging and rest run side by side across the
pan. Stay with whichever rhythm draws your eye.

11
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:34,634
Listen closely. Low nasal calls
carry across the open water.

12
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:56,445
Flamingos are obligatorily social; gentle
contact calls keep spacing across the feeding flat.

13
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:05,882
Track a single wading bird for a few paces
while the wider flock works independently.

14
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:25,975
Notice the coral coverts as it preens. That
pigment originates in the lagoon itself.

15
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:36,377
Dietary carotenoids from algae and crustaceans
are metabolized and deposited into growing feathers.

16
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:46,477
What looks like ornament is biochemical history:
the lagoon’s microscopic life made visible.

17
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:05,579
The basin looked barren when we arrived. It
reads very differently once you know where to look.

18
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:16,275
Filter feeding, single-leg rest, and quiet
flock cohesion across one shallow sheet of brine.

19
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:26,845
One desert lagoon, an intact trophic web, and
far more happening than a passing glance reveals.

20
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:46,442
Choose your next station: beneath Heron Island’s
coral reef, or beside an African savanna waterhole.
